WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.731
Building SQL or query fragments through string concatenation is a classic data access anti-pattern.

2
00:00:06.731 --> 00:00:17.066
Parameterized queries and maintained ORM patterns reduce many value-handling risks, but they do not remove the need to review the full data access path.

3
00:00:17.066 --> 00:00:19.310
Parameterization protects values.

4
00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:30.665
It does not automatically make every dynamic table name, sort field, filter expression, projection, stored procedure call, raw SQL block, or query builder option safe.

5
00:00:30.665 --> 00:00:41.407
When user or request input influences query shape, the application should map that input to known choices instead of treating it as free-form query structure.

6
00:00:41.407 --> 00:00:50.178
ORMs help, but they do not automatically enforce tenant boundaries, resource ownership, authorization rules, or safe projections.

7
00:00:50.178 --> 00:00:53.170
A LINQ query can still return too much data.

8
00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:56.570
A repository method can still skip a policy check.

9
00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:02.281
A convenient Include can still pull sensitive related records into a response model.

10
00:01:02.281 --> 00:01:12.276
High-risk workflows deserve extra review: export, reporting, administration, search, bulk update, migration, reconciliation, and dynamic filtering.

11
00:01:12.276 --> 00:01:22.271
These features often need flexible data access, which makes ownership, filtering, projection, and authorization decisions more important, not less.

12
00:01:22.271 --> 00:01:25.671
Database identities should follow least privilege.

13
00:01:25.671 --> 00:01:34.578
An application account that only needs to read and update a bounded set of tables should not have broad administrative permissions.

14
00:01:34.578 --> 00:01:42.737
Separation between runtime access, migration access, reporting access, and operational maintenance helps limit mistakes.

15
00:01:42.737 --> 00:01:52.052
Errors and logs should not expose SQL text with sensitive values, connection strings, internal schema details, or provider configuration.

16
00:01:52.052 --> 00:02:03.270
Useful diagnostics can identify the operation, failure category, correlation ID, and safe resource identifiers without recording details that should remain internal.
